keep-data: the old system Debian GNU/Linux 12 (bookworm) (old.example.net) on /dev/mapper/vg0-root; UEFI firmware; the layout of the profile is not applied, no disk is wiped or partitioned
FORMAT (what is on them is lost)
  /dev/mapper/vg0-root  ext4   /  (10G)
  /dev/vda2             ext4   /boot  (1G)
KEEP (as they are, mounted where they were)
  /dev/vda1             vfat   /boot/efi  (the EFI system partition: not formatted, the new boot loader is added)
  /dev/mapper/vg0-swap  swap   swap
  /dev/mapper/vg0-data  xfs    /data
  /dev/vda4             ext4   /srv/plain
  tmpfs                 tmpfs  /tmp
  /data/varlib          none   /var/lib.old  (at /var/lib in the old system: the new system's own is on the root)
  /dev/vda4             ext4   /home/shared
ASIDE (kept, under another name)
  /dev/mapper/vg0-var   ext4   /var -> /var.old  (mounted)
UNUSED (left as they are, not mounted by the new system)
  /data/etc             none   /etc
  nas.example.net:/usr  nfs    /usr
LOST (on the old root, beyond the system itself)
  /backup  2M
  /etc  6M
  /home/alice  1.1G
  /home/bob  1M
  /opt  245M
  /root  1M
  /usr/local  69M
  /var/lib/apt  176M
  /var/lib/docker  8.5G
WARNINGS
  /dev/vda4 (/home/shared) is named as the old system's kernel named it; here that is /dev/vda4 (ext4, label plain, 8G): taken for the volume meant, and named by its UUID from now on
  /dev/sr0 (/media/cdrom0) of the old fstab is dropped: the device is not there
  /home/shared is kept: the profile's users get their home directories and keys there; a home directory that is there already goes to the user of its name (its files change owner where the user's ID differs), its authorized_keys is replaced (the old one saved beside it)
  /data/etc (/etc, none) of the old fstab is not mounted by the new system, whose own /etc is on the root
  nas.example.net:/usr (/usr, nfs) of the old fstab is not mounted by the new system, whose own /usr is on the root
  /data/varlib (/var/lib, none) of the old fstab is mounted at /var/lib.old: the new system's own /var/lib is on the root
--- /etc/fstab
# /etc/fstab, written by UMP (keep-data: the root made anew, the old system's other volumes as they were)
UUID=NEW-vg0-root  /  ext4  defaults,errors=remount-ro  0 1
UUID=NEW-vda2  /boot  ext4  defaults  0 2
UUID=769B-CE7F  /boot/efi  vfat  umask=0077  0 1
/dev/mapper/vg0-swap  none  swap  sw  0 0
/dev/mapper/vg0-var  /var.old  ext4  defaults  0 2
/dev/vg0/data  /data  xfs  defaults,noatime  0 2
LABEL=plain  /srv/plain  ext4  defaults  0 2
tmpfs  /tmp  tmpfs  nosuid,nodev,size=1G  0 0
/data/varlib  /var/lib.old  none  bind  0 0
UUID=0def8361-4fc3-5016-a46a-7427924b4dd8  /home/shared  ext4  defaults  0 2
